  "content": {
    "id": "p6-q22-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "22",
    "prompt": "In a particular mathematics class, 52% of the students are boys. When five boys leave the class, 40% of the students are now boys. How many students were originally in the class?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "25 students.\n\nEquate the remaining boys to 40% of the remaining students.",
      "worked": "Let $n$ be the original number of students and $b$ the original number of boys. Then $b=0.52n$. After five boys leave,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{b-5}{n-5}&=0.4\\\\b-5&=0.4(n-5)\\\\0.52n-5&=0.4(n-5)\\\\52n-500&=40(n-5)\\\\52n-500&=40n-200\\\\12n&=300\\\\n&=\\frac{300}{12}\\\\n&=25.\\end{align*}$$\nThere were originally 25 students in the class.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 94
  },
